Yup.  But just in case there is a tie at the end, the proper way to 
handle it is definitely *not* to give all tied players full credit *plus* 
allow a full amount of runner ups.  This kind of thing comes up in real-
world sporting events, and there are a couple good ways to handle it:

- Results-based tie breaker, such as "points scored" or "points allowed" 
in football.  A good tie breaker here would be giving a tied award to the 
player who completed the requirements for it first.

- If N players are tied for place X, the total points for places X 
through X+N-1 are divided evenly among them, and the next runner up is 
awarded place X+N.


